From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 23:24:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/smmstore: Lock of TCG Secure MOR in SMM

Revision 2 of the TCG Platform Reset Attack Mitigation specification adds
MemoryOverwriteRequestControlLock: an OS that has set its request to
clear the memory at the next boot (bit 0 of MemoryOverwriteRequestControl)
locks it, so that code that runs in the OS after it cannot clear the
request and then reset to read the secrets left in memory. Windows uses
it for Credential Guard. The lock is only worth anything where the OS
cannot write the variable store but through the firmware: in EDK2 it is
in SMM, around the variable driver. A coreboot payload's variable driver
runs outside SMM, so the lock has to be here.

SMMSTORE_CMD_MOR_LOCK, for the payload's variable driver: lock, with or
without a key, and unlock with the key, as the specification has it (a
wrong key drops the key: locked until the next boot). While the lock
holds and the OS had asked for the clearing when it locked (or had no
variable, which counts as asking), every raw write and erase of this
handler that touches the SMMSTORE region is first applied to a view of
the variable store as the flash would be after it (programmed: bits are
only cleared; erased: 0xff), and refused when efi_fv_get_mor(), which the
decision to clear the memory at boot now reads too, would then find the
request gone. Other variables are written as before. The data is copied
into SMRAM before it is checked and written from there.

The state is in a small SMRAM region kept across S3
(SMM_MOR_LOCK_STATE_SMRAM, below the OPAL S3 state), zeroed on every
other boot, as the lock lasts until the next boot.

Only with SMM-only flash writes (enable_smm_bios_protection(), BIOS
Lock): otherwise the OS writes the flash without SMM, and the command
says the lock is unsupported. SMMSTORE_MOR_LOCK_ANY_BIOS_LOCK offers it
anyway, for tests in QEMU.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
